#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-guard-messages — enforce the LLM-facing guard-message convention.
#
# Every first-party guard / hook / check / gate-wrapper that can DENY or FAIL
# must tell the agent HOW TO SELF-CORRECT, not just that it failed. The
# convention (see ~/dev/custom/CLAUDE.md -> "Guard/error messages are written
# for the LLM") is: such output carries the greppable marker `Fix:` followed by
# an actionable instruction. `check-generic-skills` and the safe-wait /
# pronoun guards are the exemplars.
#
# SCOPE IS EVERY FIRST-PARTY EXECUTABLE, AND EVERY ONE IS CLASSIFIED (DND-218)
#
# This check used to scan ai/hooks/*.sh plus a hand-kept GUARD_BINS list under
# ai/bin/. Nothing under scripts/, git-custom/, or a skill's bin/ was read, and
# an ai/bin guard missing from the list was never read either -- yet the check
# printed OK. A check whose scope silently excludes a directory reports success
# for files it never looked at.
#
# So discovery is no longer a list of places to look. It is the repo itself:
# every file `git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard` returns (minus
# untracked third-party trees, see the section below) that is executable, or
# lives under a `lib/` / `wt-lib/` directory (sourced code), or is an
# ai/hooks/*.sh. Each discovered file must have exactly one class:
#
#   guard         must carry `Fix:`. Every file under GUARD_DIRS (ai/hooks,
#                 ai/bin) defaults to this, so a new hook or bin is covered
#                 without anyone remembering to list it.
#   tool          a first-party utility that performs an action; it is not a
#                 guard, check, or gate, and its failures are usage/precondition
#                 errors. Reason required.
#   no-fail-path  has no deny/failure path at all. Reason required.
#   library       sourced code, never an entry point. Reason required.
#   test          a test suite (a `test/` path segment or `*.self-test.sh`);
#                 decided by rule, see TEST_REASON.
#
# UNTRACKED THIRD-PARTY TREES ARE NOT FIRST-PARTY (DND-512)
#
# Discovery has two sources, and they are not treated alike:
#   - TRACKED paths (`git ls-files --cached`: committed or staged) are always
#     candidates. Anything in the index is repo content, even inside a
#     node_modules/ -- a vendored tree someone commits must be classified.
#   - UNTRACKED paths (`--others --exclude-standard`) are candidates only as
#     NEW first-party files. An untracked path with a THIRD_PARTY_SEGMENTS
#     segment (node_modules, .venv) or a vendor/bundle segment pair is a
#     package manager's install output, not first-party code, and is skipped.
#     The skip is counted in the OK/FAILED output, never silent.
# Why: the main checkout held an untracked, un-ignored node_modules tree
# (ai/skills/athena:inbox/channel/node_modules). Its executables and lib/ files
# read as 182 unclassified first-party files, and the gate went red in the main
# checkout while every clean worktree stayed green.
# The match is on whole path segments: node_modules-tools/, a bare vendor/,
# .venv-notes/ are first-party and still discovered.
# Residual: an untracked first-party script placed INSIDE such a segment is not
# read until it is staged (`git add`) -- at which point it is a tracked
# candidate and must be classified. Nothing is skipped once it can be committed.
#
# Per-file classes live in ai/guard-classification.tsv. The shape is an
# ALLOWLIST on purpose: a new script outside GUARD_DIRS that nobody classified
# is the drift, and it FAILS here. The check also fails when:
#   - discovery found nothing, or a GUARD_DIR has no files (cannot measure);
#   - a classified path no longer exists (the scope would silently shrink);
#   - the table is missing or malformed, or a non-guard has no reason.
#
# THE TABLE'S BAR IS THE LANDED TABLE, NOT THIS DIFF'S (DND-510)
#
# The table lives in the same diff this check judges. Before DND-510 a change
# could relabel a real guard as tool, delete its Fix: line, and stay green: the
# check compared the change against the bar the change had just written. See
# ~/dev/custom/CLAUDE.md -> "A check's own bar must not live in the diff it is
# checking".
#
# So every run also reads the LANDED classification out of git, at two points:
# the tip of refs/remotes/origin/main and merge-base(HEAD, origin/main). A path's
# landed class is the STRICTEST across both (guard at either point is guard), so
# neither a stale origin/main nor an old merge-base lets a relabel through.
# A WEAKENING fails:
#   - a path that was a guard at a landed point is now any non-guard class --
#     relabelled, newly listed under a GUARD_DIR, or its guard entry removed so
#     it falls back to a rule class (test) or to unclassified;
#   - a landed guard whose file still exists is no longer discovered at all
#     (it lost its exec bit, or became a symlink).
# Tightening (non-guard -> guard) passes. A GENUINELY NEW entry -- a path that
# was not a first-party candidate at any landed point -- passes and is named in
# the OK output, so a reviewer sees every new non-guard label. Deleting a
# landed guard outright is a removal, not a reclassification, and passes.
#
# A MOVED GUARD KEEPS ITS BAR (DND-539)
#
# The ratchet above keys on PATH. Before DND-539, a landed guard deleted at its
# old path and re-added at a new one read as a removal plus a genuinely new
# entry, so the new copy could be labelled tool, lose its Fix: line, and pass.
# So a NEW candidate whose content maps to a REMOVED landed guard (its path no
# longer exists) inherits that guard's bar: if it is not a guard now, that is a
# weakening, named with both paths. "Maps" is git's own rename detection,
# `git diff --no-index -M50%`, over the removed guards' landed blobs (every
# distinct blob at either landed point) and the new candidates' working-tree
# content, copied to a temp dir so the repo is only read. That covers the same
# blob (an exact rename) and an edited move alike, tracked or untracked.
#
# Why 50%: it is git's default, the definition of "this file was moved" that
# `git diff -M`, `git log --follow`, merge rename handling, and the forge's
# rename display all use. A reviewer who sees "renamed" in the diff sees the
# same verdict here. Lower thresholds match small scripts that share only a
# shebang, `set -euo pipefail`, and usage boilerplate, and would fail genuinely
# new tools. Higher ones let a guard through after light edits (its Fix: lines
# deleted is already a few percent). No threshold stops a full rewrite; see the
# residual below. `-l0` lifts git's rename limit, so a large diff cannot
# silently skip detection. A detection that fails is "could not measure".
#
# Not being able to read the landed table is a FAILURE, never a pass: no
# origin/main, no merge-base, a shallow clone, or a landed commit without a
# readable table. The output lists every probe and what it gave, and keeps
# "could not measure" textually distinct from "0 weakening(s)".
#
# There is no in-repo escape hatch -- no env var, approved list, or marker --
# because anything this check could read, the diff under test could also write.
# The hatch is outside the diff: the OWNER lands the reclassification on main,
# and a branch rebased onto it passes.
#
# Why here and not a sibling check: the ratchet must judge the landed table
# with the SAME rules the check applies now (the GUARD_DIRS default, the test
# rule, the table parser, candidate discovery). A sibling would duplicate those
# rules, and two copies that drift apart are a second silent hole. It also
# costs one extra rev-parse/merge-base and two `git show` + `git ls-tree` per
# run, and harness-gate already runs this check.
#
# Residual, stated out loud: this does not stop an actor that can push to main
# (it can land the relabel itself), nor a diff that edits this file's
# enforcement path. A guard moved to a new path is caught while git's rename
# detection still pairs the two (>= 50% similar); one rewritten beyond that
# reads as a removal plus a new entry: it passes, and the new entry is named in
# the OK output. Git pairs each removed guard with at most one new file, so a
# guard copied to two new paths is judged by the copy git picks. A guard moved
# to a path discovery does not read (not executable, not under lib/ or
# ai/hooks) is not a candidate there and reads as a removal.
#
# Convention idea adapted from riddler's howie/wurk harness ("errors written for
# the LLM"), reimplemented in our conventions.
#
# Deliberately gem-free (stdlib only); run with bare `ruby`.
#
# Usage:
#   ai/bin/check-guard-messages              classify + scan + ratchet against the
#                                            landed table; exit 1 on any gap
#   ai/bin/check-guard-messages --root DIR   measure the checkout at DIR
#                                            (read-only; default: this repo)
#   ai/bin/check-guard-messages --self-test  verify the checker's own logic
#
# The black-box coverage suite is ai/test/check-guard-messages/self-test.sh.

require "fileutils"
require "open3"
require "tmpdir"

REPO  = File.expand_path("../..", __dir__)
TABLE = "ai/guard-classification.tsv"

MARKER = "Fix:"

# Every discovered file under these directories is a guard unless the table
# classifies it otherwise.
GUARD_DIRS = %w[ai/hooks ai/bin].freeze

# Sourced code is discovered even though it is not executable.
LIB_SEGMENTS = %w[lib wt-lib].freeze

# Package-manager install directories. An UNTRACKED path with one of these
# segments is third-party and skipped; a tracked one is still a candidate.
THIRD_PARTY_SEGMENTS  = %w[node_modules .venv].freeze
# Consecutive segment pairs with the same meaning (Bundler's --path default).
THIRD_PARTY_SEQUENCES = [%w[vendor bundle]].freeze
THIRD_PARTY_RULE = "untracked paths under a node_modules/, .venv/, or " \
                   "vendor/bundle/ segment"

CLASSES     = %w[guard tool no-fail-path library].freeze
MIN_REASON  = 20

TEST_REASON = "test suite: its failure output is the failing case and its " \
              "assertion; ai/bin/harness-gate, which runs every suite, is the " \
              "gate-wrapper and carries Fix:"

# Raised when the check cannot measure. Always surfaced with a Fix:, never as a
# backtrace and never as a pass.
MeasureError = Class.new(StandardError)

def rel_segments(rel)
  rel.split("/")
end

def test_path?(rel)
  segs = rel_segments(rel)
  segs[0..-2].include?("test") || segs.last.end_with?(".self-test.sh")
end

def lib_path?(rel)
  (rel_segments(rel)[0..-2] & LIB_SEGMENTS).any?
end

def hook_script?(rel)
  rel.start_with?("ai/hooks/") && rel.end_with?(".sh")
end

def third_party_path?(rel)
  dirs = rel_segments(rel)[0..-2]
  return true if (dirs & THIRD_PARTY_SEGMENTS).any?

  THIRD_PARTY_SEQUENCES.any? { |seq| dirs.each_cons(seq.size).include?(seq) }
end

def guard_dir_path?(rel)
  GUARD_DIRS.any? { |d| rel.start_with?("#{d}/") }
end

# Paths git lists for one ls-files mode. Not being able to ask git is a
# measurement failure, not an empty tree.
def git_ls(root, *mode)
  out, err, status = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "-z", *mode)
  raise MeasureError, "git -C #{root} ls-files #{mode.join(' ')} failed: #{err.strip}" unless status.success?

  out.split("\0").uniq
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  raise MeasureError, "git executable not found on PATH"
end

def candidate?(root, rel)
  abs = File.join(root, rel)
  return false if File.symlink?(abs) || !File.file?(abs)

  File.executable?(abs) || lib_path?(rel) || hook_script?(rel)
end

# Returns [found, skipped]: found is every first-party candidate; skipped is
# every untracked third-party path that would otherwise have been one.
def discover(root)
  tracked = git_ls(root, "--cached")
  untracked = git_ls(root, "--others", "--exclude-standard") - tracked
  third_party, new_files = untracked.partition { |rel| third_party_path?(rel) }
  found = (tracked + new_files).uniq.select { |rel| candidate?(root, rel) }.sort
  [found, third_party.select { |rel| candidate?(root, rel) }.sort]
end

# Parse the TSV. Returns [entries, errors]; entries maps path -> [class, reason].
def load_table(root)
  path = File.join(root, TABLE)
  raise MeasureError, "#{TABLE} is missing or unreadable" unless File.file?(path) && File.readable?(path)

  parse_table(File.read(path), TABLE)
end

# Parse table TEXT (the working tree's, or a landed commit's), so the ratchet
# reads the landed table with exactly the rules the check applies now.
def parse_table(text, source)
  entries = {}
  errors = []
  text.to_s.each_line(chomp: true).with_index do |line, i|
    next if line.strip.empty? || line.start_with?("#")

    rel, klass, reason, *extra = line.split("\t", -1)
    where = "#{source}:#{i + 1}"
    reason = reason.to_s.strip
    if rel.to_s.empty? || klass.nil? || !extra.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: expected 'path<TAB>class<TAB>reason', got #{line.inspect}"
    elsif !CLASSES.include?(klass)
      errors << "#{where}: #{rel}: unknown class #{klass.inspect} (one of #{CLASSES.join(', ')})"
    elsif klass != "guard" && reason.length < MIN_REASON
      errors << "#{where}: #{rel}: class #{klass} needs a real reason (why it has no " \
                "guard failure path), got #{reason.inspect}"
    elsif entries.key?(rel)
      errors << "#{where}: #{rel}: classified twice"
    else
      entries[rel] = [klass, reason]
    end
  end
  [entries, errors]
end

def classify(rel, entries)
  return entries[rel][0] if entries.key?(rel)
  return "test" if test_path?(rel)
  return "guard" if guard_dir_path?(rel)

  nil
end

# A guard is compliant if its source contains the Fix: marker somewhere in its
# output. (Static heuristic: the actionable clause is present to be emitted.)
def compliant?(path)
  File.read(path).include?(MARKER)
end

def scan(paths)
  paths.reject { |p| compliant?(p) }
end

# The whole evaluation, as data, so --self-test can drive it on fixtures.
def evaluate(root)
  found, skipped = discover(root)
  raise MeasureError, "discovered 0 first-party files under #{root}" if found.empty?

  entries, table_errors = load_table(root)
  empty_dirs = GUARD_DIRS.reject { |d| found.any? { |rel| rel.start_with?("#{d}/") } }
  by_class = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }
  unclassified = []
  found.each do |rel|
    klass = classify(rel, entries)
    klass ? by_class[klass] << rel : unclassified << rel
  end
  {
    found: found,
    skipped: skipped,
    table_errors: table_errors,
    empty_dirs: empty_dirs,
    unclassified: unclassified,
    vanished: entries.keys.reject { |rel| found.include?(rel) }.sort,
    missing_fix: scan(by_class["guard"].map { |rel| File.join(root, rel) })
                   .map { |abs| abs.delete_prefix("#{root}/") },
    by_class: by_class,
  }
end

# --- the classification ratchet (DND-510) ------------------------------------

LANDED_REF = "refs/remotes/origin/main"

# The landed bar could not be read. Carries every probe and what it gave, so
# "could not look" is printed as a list of places looked, never as a clean run.
class LandedUnreadable < StandardError
  attr_reader :probes

  def initialize(probes)
    @probes = probes
    super("the landed classification could not be read")
  end
end

def git_read(root, *args)
  out, _err, status = Open3.capture3({ "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS" => "0" }, "git", "-C", root, *args)
  [out, status.success?]
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  ["", false]
end

# Was rel a discovery candidate at a landed commit? Mirrors candidate?: an
# executable blob, or a lib/ or ai/hooks/*.sh blob. Symlinks (120000) and
# submodules (160000) never are.
def landed_candidate?(mode, rel)
  return true if mode == "100755"

  mode == "100644" && (lib_path?(rel) || hook_script?(rel))
end

# rel -> landed class at one commit, for every landed candidate there. When
# blobs is given, it also collects rel -> [blob sha, ...] for those candidates,
# so a guard removed at its path can be traced to where its content went.
def classes_at(root, sha, label, blobs = nil)
  text, ok = git_read(root, "show", "#{sha}:#{TABLE}")
  raise LandedUnreadable.new([[label, "#{sha[0, 12]}: no readable #{TABLE}"]]) unless ok

  entries, errors = parse_table(text, "#{label}:#{TABLE}")
  unless errors.empty?
    raise LandedUnreadable.new([[label, "#{sha[0, 12]}: landed #{TABLE} is malformed (#{errors.first})"]])
  end

  tree, ok = git_read(root, "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", sha)
  raise LandedUnreadable.new([[label, "#{sha[0, 12]}: git ls-tree failed"]]) unless ok

  tree.split("\0").each_with_object({}) do |row, classes|
    meta, rel = row.split("\t", 2)
    next if rel.nil?

    mode, _type, blob = meta.split(" ")
    next unless landed_candidate?(mode, rel)

    klass = classify(rel, entries)
    next unless klass

    classes[rel] = klass
    (blobs[rel] ||= []) << blob if blobs && !blobs.fetch(rel, []).include?(blob)
  end
end

# The strictest class per path across points: guard at any point wins.
def merge_landed(per_point)
  per_point.each_with_object({}) do |classes, merged|
    classes.each do |rel, klass|
      merged[rel] = klass if merged[rel].nil? || klass == "guard"
    end
  end
end

# Resolve the landed points and read each one's classification. Every miss
# raises LandedUnreadable naming what was probed. Returns [points, classes,
# blobs], blobs mapping each landed candidate to its blob(s) across the points.
def landed_classification(root)
  probes = []
  unreadable = lambda do |ref, outcome|
    probes << [ref, outcome]
    raise LandedUnreadable.new(probes)
  end

  tip, ok = git_read(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "#{LANDED_REF}^{commit}")
  tip = tip.strip
  unreadable.call(LANDED_REF, "no such ref") if !ok || tip.empty?
  probes << [LANDED_REF, tip[0, 12]]

  shallow, ok = git_read(root, "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository")
  unreadable.call("git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository", "failed") unless ok
  if shallow.strip == "true"
    unreadable.call("history depth", "shallow clone: the merge-base with origin/main cannot be proven")
  end

  mb, ok = git_read(root, "merge-base", "HEAD", tip)
  mb = mb.strip
  unreadable.call("merge-base(HEAD, origin/main)", "no common ancestor (or no HEAD commit)") if !ok || mb.empty?
  probes << ["merge-base(HEAD, origin/main)", mb[0, 12]]

  points = if mb == tip
             [["origin/main tip = merge-base(HEAD, origin/main)", tip]]
           else
             [["origin/main (tip)", tip], ["merge-base(HEAD, origin/main)", mb]]
           end
  blobs = {}
  per_point = points.map do |label, sha|
    classes_at(root, sha, label, blobs)
  rescue LandedUnreadable => e
    raise LandedUnreadable.new(probes + e.probes)
  end
  [points, merge_landed(per_point), blobs]
end

# [rel, landed, now] for every landed guard that is no longer a guard. A landed
# guard absent from discovery counts only when its file still exists: deleting
# it is a removal, losing the exec bit is a weakening.
def weakenings(root, found, entries, landed)
  found_set = found.to_h { |rel| [rel, true] }
  landed.filter_map do |rel, klass|
    next nil unless klass == "guard"

    if found_set[rel]
      now = classify(rel, entries)
      now == "guard" ? nil : [rel, klass, now || "unclassified"]
    elsif File.exist?(File.join(root, rel)) || File.symlink?(File.join(root, rel))
      [rel, klass, "no longer discovered (lost its exec bit, or became a symlink)"]
    end
  end.sort
end

# --- a guard moved to a new path keeps its bar (DND-539) ---------------------

# git's own default rename threshold. See "A MOVED GUARD" in the header.
RENAME_SIMILARITY = 50
RENAME_PROBE = "git diff --no-index -M#{RENAME_SIMILARITY}% (rename detection)".freeze

# Pairs each REMOVED landed guard (its path no longer exists) with the NEW
# candidate its content moved to, using git's rename detection: the same blob,
# or >= RENAME_SIMILARITY% similar. Returns [weakened, kept]:
#   weakened  [new_rel, "guard at <old> (moved, R<score>)", now] where the new
#             path is not a guard;
#   kept      [old_rel, new_rel, score] where it still is.
# Git pairs each source with at most one destination (exact matches first,
# then best score). A failed detection raises LandedUnreadable: it is a missing
# measurement, never "nothing moved".
def moved_guards(root, found, entries, landed, blobs)
  removed = landed.select do |rel, klass|
    klass == "guard" && !File.exist?(File.join(root, rel)) && !File.symlink?(File.join(root, rel))
  end.keys.sort
  added = found.reject { |rel| landed.key?(rel) }
  return [[], []] if removed.empty? || added.empty?

  pairs = rename_pairs(root, removed.flat_map { |rel| blobs.fetch(rel, []).map { |b| [rel, b] } }, added)
  weakened = []
  kept = []
  pairs.each do |old_rel, new_rel, score|
    now = classify(new_rel, entries)
    if now == "guard"
      kept << [old_rel, new_rel, score]
    else
      weakened << [new_rel, "guard at #{old_rel} (moved here, #{score}% similar)", now || "unclassified"]
    end
  end
  [weakened.sort, kept.sort]
end

# [[old_rel, new_rel, score]] for every (landed blob -> working-tree file) pair
# git's rename detection finds. The comparison runs on copies in a temp dir, so
# the repo is only read: no index, no object store, no ref is written.
def rename_pairs(root, sources, added)
  Dir.mktmpdir("check-guard-messages-moved-") do |tmp|
    src_names = write_rename_side(File.join(tmp, "src"), "s", sources) do |(_rel, blob)|
      text, ok = git_read(root, "cat-file", "blob", blob)
      raise LandedUnreadable.new([[RENAME_PROBE, "git cat-file blob #{blob[0, 12]} failed"]]) unless ok

      text
    end
    dst_names = write_rename_side(File.join(tmp, "dst"), "d", added) { |rel| File.binread(File.join(root, rel)) }
    out, err, status = Open3.capture3("git", "diff", "--no-index", "--no-ext-diff", "-M#{RENAME_SIMILARITY}%",
                                      "-l0", "--name-status", "-z", "src", "dst", chdir: tmp)
    # --no-index exits 1 both for "differences found" and for an error, so a
    # non-empty stderr is what tells them apart.
    unless [0, 1].include?(status.exitstatus) && err.strip.empty?
      raise LandedUnreadable.new([[RENAME_PROBE, "failed (exit #{status.exitstatus}): #{err.strip}"]])
    end

    parse_renames(out).map do |src, dst, score|
      [src_names.fetch(src)[0], dst_names.fetch(dst), score]
    end
  end
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  raise LandedUnreadable.new([[RENAME_PROBE, "git executable not found on PATH"]])
end

# Writes one file per item under dir, named <prefix><n>, from the block's
# content. Returns { "<side>/<name>" => item }.
def write_rename_side(dir, prefix, items)
  FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir)
  side = File.basename(dir)
  items.each_with_index.to_h do |item, i|
    name = format("%s%05d", prefix, i)
    File.binwrite(File.join(dir, name), yield(item))
    ["#{side}/#{name}", item]
  end
end

# [[src, dst, score]] from `--name-status -z` output: "R<score>\0src\0dst\0".
def parse_renames(out)
  tokens = out.split("\0")
  renames = []
  until tokens.empty?
    status = tokens.shift
    paths = tokens.shift(status.start_with?("R", "C") ? 2 : 1)
    renames << [paths[0], paths[1], status[1..].to_i] if status.start_with?("R")
  end
  renames
end

# Table entries for paths that were not a candidate at any landed point.
def new_entries(entries, landed)
  entries.reject { |rel, _| landed.key?(rel) }.map { |rel, (klass, _)| [rel, klass] }.sort
end

# Adds the ratchet's findings to an evaluate() result.
def ratchet!(root, result)
  entries, = load_table(root)
  points, landed, blobs = landed_classification(root)
  result[:landed_points] = points
  moved, kept = moved_guards(root, result[:found], entries, landed, blobs)
  result[:weakened] = (weakenings(root, result[:found], entries, landed) + moved).sort
  result[:moved_kept] = kept
  result[:new_entries] = new_entries(entries, landed)
rescue LandedUnreadable => e
  result[:landed_probes] = e.probes
ensure
  result[:weakened] ||= []
end

def failed?(result)
  %i[table_errors empty_dirs unclassified vanished missing_fix].any? { |k| result[k].any? } ||
    result.fetch(:weakened, []).any? || result.key?(:landed_probes)
end

def skipped_note(result)
  "#{result[:skipped].size} untracked third-party file(s) skipped (#{THIRD_PARTY_RULE})"
end

def report_failure(result)
  warn "check-guard-messages: FAILED (#{skipped_note(result)})"
  unless result[:table_errors].empty?
    warn "  malformed #{TABLE}:"
    result[:table_errors].each { |e| warn "    - #{e}" }
    warn "  Fix: one line per file, 'path<TAB>class<TAB>reason'; class is one of " \
         "#{CLASSES.join(', ')}; every class but guard states why the file has no " \
         "guard failure path."
  end
  unless result[:empty_dirs].empty?
    warn "  guard director(ies) with ZERO discovered files: #{result[:empty_dirs].join(', ')}"
    warn "  Fix: a guard directory that reads empty cannot be measured; restore it, " \
         "or remove it from GUARD_DIRS in ai/bin/check-guard-messages if it was retired."
  end
  unless result[:unclassified].empty?
    warn "  unclassified first-party file(s) -- the checker cannot tell whether they must carry '#{MARKER}':"
    result[:unclassified].each { |rel| warn "    - #{rel}" }
    warn "  Fix: add each to #{TABLE}: 'guard' if it denies, checks, or gates " \
         "(then give it a '#{MARKER}' line), else tool / no-fail-path / library with the " \
         "real reason. Never classify a guard as anything else to make this pass. " \
         "Only #{THIRD_PARTY_RULE} are skipped as third-party; never move a " \
         "first-party script into one to hide it -- it is read again once staged."
  end
  unless result[:vanished].empty?
    warn "  classified file(s) that no longer exist (or lost their exec bit):"
    result[:vanished].each { |rel| warn "    - #{rel}" }
    warn "  Fix: if the file was deleted or renamed, update its line in #{TABLE}; " \
         "if it should exist, restore it."
  end
  unless result[:missing_fix].empty?
    warn "  guard(s) with a bare failure message (no '#{MARKER}' self-correction):"
    result[:missing_fix].each { |rel| warn "    - #{rel}" }
    warn "  Fix: add a '#{MARKER} <what to change to pass>' clause to each guard's " \
         "deny/failure output. Reclassify one only if it truly has no guard failure " \
         "path, with that reason, in #{TABLE}."
  end
  report_ratchet_failure(result)
end

def report_ratchet_failure(result)
  if result.key?(:landed_probes)
    warn "  could not measure the landed classification, so no weakening could be detected."
    warn "  This is a missing MEASUREMENT, not a clean result. Probed, and what each gave:"
    result[:landed_probes].each { |ref, outcome| warn "    #{ref} -> #{outcome}" }
    warn "  Fix: give the check a landed table to compare against: `git fetch origin main` " \
         "(so #{LANDED_REF} exists and shares history with HEAD), or `git fetch --unshallow` " \
         "in a shallow clone. Run from a branch that descends from origin/main."
  end
  return if result[:weakened].empty?

  warn "  classification(s) weakened below the landed bar (strictest of origin/main tip and merge-base):"
  result[:weakened].each { |rel, landed, now| warn "    - #{rel}: landed #{landed} -> now #{now}" }
  warn "  This diff moved its own bar: the table is in the change this check judges, so a " \
       "relabel here is self-satisfying."
  warn "  Fix: restore each path to 'guard' in #{TABLE} (and executable, if it lost its exec " \
       "bit), and give it a '#{MARKER}' line. If the reclassification is genuinely right, " \
       "the OWNER lands it on main themselves; rebase onto it and this passes. There is no " \
       "in-repo approval flag, env var, or marker. If main already landed it after you " \
       "forked, rebase onto current origin/main."
end

def run_check(root = REPO)
  result = evaluate(root)
  ratchet!(root, result)
  if failed?(result)
    report_failure(result)
    return 1
  end

  counts = (%w[guard] + CLASSES.drop(1) + %w[test])
           .map { |k| "#{result[:by_class][k].size} #{k}" }.join(", ")
  puts "check-guard-messages: OK — #{result[:found].size} first-party file(s) " \
       "classified (#{counts}); every guard carries an actionable '#{MARKER}'; " \
       "#{skipped_note(result)}"
  puts "  classification ratchet: 0 weakening(s) against " +
       result[:landed_points].map { |label, sha| "#{label} @ #{sha[0, 12]}" }.join(", ")
  unless result[:moved_kept].empty?
    puts "  landed guard(s) moved to a new path, still guard: " +
         result[:moved_kept].map { |old, new, score| "#{old} -> #{new} (#{score}% similar)" }.join(", ")
  end
  unless result[:new_entries].empty?
    puts "  new classification entr(ies) with no landed counterpart: " +
         result[:new_entries].map { |rel, klass| "#{rel} (#{klass})" }.join(", ")
  end
  0
rescue MeasureError => e
  warn "check-guard-messages: FAILED — could not measure: #{e.message}"
  warn "  Fix: run from a git checkout of ~/dev/custom with git on PATH and " \
       "#{TABLE} present; a check that cannot look must not report OK."
  1
end

def run_self_test
  dir = Dir.mktmpdir("check-guard-messages-selftest-")
  ok = true
  fail = ->(msg) { warn "self-test: FAIL — #{msg}"; ok = false }
  begin
    good = File.join(dir, "good.sh")
    bad  = File.join(dir, "bad.sh")
    File.write(good, "#!/bin/sh\necho 'DENY: bad thing. Fix: do the good thing.'\n")
    File.write(bad,  "#!/bin/sh\necho 'failed'\n")
    fail.("a guard WITH '#{MARKER}' was flagged") if scan([good]).any?
    fail.("a guard WITHOUT '#{MARKER}' was not flagged") if scan([bad]).empty?

    # Rules, as pure predicates.
    fail.("scripts/test/x/self-test.sh not a test") unless test_path?("scripts/test/x/self-test.sh")
    fail.("ai/hooks/a.self-test.sh not a test") unless test_path?("ai/hooks/a.self-test.sh")
    fail.("scripts/test-required.sh read as a test") if test_path?("scripts/test-required.sh")
    fail.("scripts/lib/x.sh not a lib") unless lib_path?("scripts/lib/x.sh")
    fail.("scripts/libx read as a lib") if lib_path?("scripts/libx")
    fail.("a/node_modules/b/bin/x not third-party") unless third_party_path?("a/node_modules/b/bin/x")
    fail.("a/.venv/bin/x not third-party") unless third_party_path?("a/.venv/bin/x")
    fail.("vendor/bundle/ruby/bin/x not third-party") unless third_party_path?("vendor/bundle/ruby/bin/x")
    fail.("scripts/node_modules-x/run read as third-party") if third_party_path?("scripts/node_modules-x/run")
    fail.("vendor/mine/run read as third-party") if third_party_path?("vendor/mine/run")
    fail.("a file NAMED node_modules read as third-party") if third_party_path?("scripts/node_modules")

    # DND-510 ratchet rules, as pure functions (the black-box suite drives them
    # against real fixture repos with a fake origin/main).
    fail.("100755 blob not a landed candidate") unless landed_candidate?("100755", "scripts/x")
    fail.("100644 lib blob not a landed candidate") unless landed_candidate?("100644", "scripts/lib/x.sh")
    fail.("100644 hook not a landed candidate") unless landed_candidate?("100644", "ai/hooks/x.sh")
    fail.("symlink read as a landed candidate") if landed_candidate?("120000", "ai/bin/x")
    fail.("plain 100644 read as a landed candidate") if landed_candidate?("100644", "ai/bin/README")
    merged = merge_landed([{ "a" => "tool", "b" => "guard" }, { "a" => "guard", "b" => "tool", "c" => "tool" }])
    unless merged == { "a" => "guard", "b" => "guard", "c" => "tool" }
      fail.("merge_landed must keep guard from ANY point: got #{merged.inspect}")
    end
    w = weakenings(dir, %w[ai/bin/x scripts/y scripts/z], { "ai/bin/x" => ["tool", "r" * 20] },
                   { "ai/bin/x" => "guard", "scripts/y" => "tool", "scripts/z" => "guard", "gone" => "guard" })
    unless w == [["ai/bin/x", "guard", "tool"], ["scripts/z", "guard", "unclassified"]]
      fail.("weakenings: got #{w.inspect}")
    end
    renames = parse_renames("R100\0src/s00000\0dst/d00001\0A\0dst/d00000\0R062\0src/s00001\0dst/d00002\0D\0src/s00002\0")
    unless renames == [["src/s00000", "dst/d00001", 100], ["src/s00001", "dst/d00002", 62]]
      fail.("parse_renames: got #{renames.inspect}")
    end
    fresh = new_entries({ "n" => ["tool", "r"], "o" => ["guard", ""] }, { "o" => "guard" })
    fail.("new_entries: got #{fresh.inspect}") unless fresh == [["n", "tool"]]

    # A git root that tracks nothing: an empty discovery set is an error.
    empty = File.join(dir, "empty")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(empty)
    system("git", "-C", empty, "init", "-q", exception: false)
    begin
      evaluate(empty)
      fail.("an empty discovery set did not raise")
    rescue MeasureError
      nil
    end

    # Not a git repo at all: cannot measure, never OK.
    fail.("a non-git root returned OK") if run_check_quietly(File.join(dir, "nowhere")).zero?

    # DND-422: a dot-prefixed hook is discovered (and defaults to guard); its
    # .self-test.sh is classified test.
    dot = File.join(dir, "dot")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(dot, "ai/hooks"))
    File.write(File.join(dot, TABLE), "")
    File.write(File.join(dot, "ai/hooks/.hidden-guard.sh"), "#!/bin/sh\n")
    File.write(File.join(dot, "ai/hooks/.hidden-guard.self-test.sh"), "#!/bin/sh\n")
    system("git", "-C", dot, "init", "-q", exception: false)
    r = evaluate(dot)
    unless r[:by_class]["guard"] == ["ai/hooks/.hidden-guard.sh"] &&
           r[:by_class]["test"] == ["ai/hooks/.hidden-guard.self-test.sh"]
      fail.("dot-prefixed hook discovery: got #{r[:by_class].inspect}")
    end
    fail.("a bare dot-prefixed hook was not flagged") unless r[:missing_fix] == ["ai/hooks/.hidden-guard.sh"]
  ensure
    FileUtils.rm_rf(dir)
  end

  if ok
    puts "check-guard-messages: self-test OK"
    0
  else
    warn "check-guard-messages: self-test FAILED"
    warn "  Fix: the discovery/classification logic is broken — see the FAIL " \
         "line(s) above; ai/test/check-guard-messages/self-test.sh covers the " \
         "black-box cases."
    1
  end
end

def run_check_quietly(root)
  orig = $stderr
  $stderr = File.open(File::NULL, "w")
  run_check(root)
ensure
  $stderr = orig
end

if ARGV.include?("--help") || ARGV.include?("-h")
  puts <<~TXT
    Usage:
      ai/bin/check-guard-messages              classify every first-party executable
                                               and library (#{TABLE}); assert every
                                               guard emits an actionable `Fix:`
                                               (#{THIRD_PARTY_RULE} are skipped
                                               as third-party, and counted); fail any
                                               guard weakened below the landed table
                                               (origin/main tip and merge-base),
                                               including one moved to a new path
      ai/bin/check-guard-messages --root DIR   the same, measuring the checkout at
                                               DIR read-only (its own #{TABLE})
      ai/bin/check-guard-messages --self-test  deterministic suite (no model)
      ai/bin/check-guard-messages --help       this text
  TXT
  exit 0
end
exit(run_self_test) if ARGV.include?("--self-test")

root_at = ARGV.index("--root")
if root_at
  root = ARGV[root_at + 1]
  if root.nil? || root.empty? || !File.directory?(root)
    warn "check-guard-messages: FAILED — --root needs an existing directory, got #{root.inspect}"
    warn "  Fix: pass --root <path to a git checkout>, e.g. --root ~/dev/custom."
    exit 1
  end
  exit(run_check(File.expand_path(root)))
end
exit(run_check)
